1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,405
Hello again. You
just learned a lot

2
00:00:03,405 --> 00:00:06,405
about presentation best
practices from my colleague.

3
00:00:06,405 --> 00:00:08,339
Just like you need
to be strategic,

4
00:00:08,339 --> 00:00:11,595
prepared, and clear about
which data you want to share,

5
00:00:11,595 --> 00:00:13,515
you also need to be intentional

6
00:00:13,515 --> 00:00:15,540
about ensuring your
presentations are

7
00:00:15,540 --> 00:00:17,010
accessible and can be

8
00:00:17,010 --> 00:00:19,890
consumed and understood
by everyone.

9
00:00:19,890 --> 00:00:22,350
Let's dive into some
accessibility tips

10
00:00:22,350 --> 00:00:24,030
to set you up for success,

11
00:00:24,030 --> 00:00:26,655
starting with the design
of your presentation.

12
00:00:26,655 --> 00:00:29,910
First, create clear,
simple slides.

13
00:00:29,910 --> 00:00:32,175
Avoid using too many graphics,

14
00:00:32,175 --> 00:00:35,895
too much text, or
too much animation.

15
00:00:35,895 --> 00:00:38,190
Visual complexity makes
it harder for people

16
00:00:38,190 --> 00:00:40,840
to absorb information
during your presentation,

17
00:00:40,840 --> 00:00:42,290
especially for those with

18
00:00:42,290 --> 00:00:44,690
a visual or cognitive impairment.

19
00:00:44,690 --> 00:00:47,195
If your slide uses animation,

20
00:00:47,195 --> 00:00:49,415
make sure that you don't
leave people behind

21
00:00:49,415 --> 00:00:51,905
by making important
content disappear.

22
00:00:51,905 --> 00:00:55,745
If people read more slowly
or rely on an interpreter,

23
00:00:55,745 --> 00:00:59,125
they might need a bit more
time to absorb the content.

24
00:00:59,125 --> 00:01:01,475
Avoid using repetitive animation,

25
00:01:01,475 --> 00:01:03,155
like flashing or flickering,

26
00:01:03,155 --> 00:01:06,170
since it can be distracting
and can trigger seizures.

27
00:01:06,170 --> 00:01:08,645
Simple doesn't have to
mean boring, though.

28
00:01:08,645 --> 00:01:12,140
A simple slide can still be
beautiful and informative.

29
00:01:12,140 --> 00:01:13,490
Just don't try to crowd

30
00:01:13,490 --> 00:01:17,240
too much information or
activity into a single slide.

31
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:20,570
If you generally don't use
slides when giving a talk,

32
00:01:20,570 --> 00:01:22,615
consider giving it a try—even

33
00:01:22,615 --> 00:01:26,360
if you create just one
slide with your main points.

34
00:01:26,360 --> 00:01:28,910
If you rely only on your voice,

35
00:01:28,910 --> 00:01:31,945
that is, you don't provide
any visual accompaniment,

36
00:01:31,945 --> 00:01:35,330
some people might have
difficulty understanding, whether

37
00:01:35,330 --> 00:01:36,965
because of a language barrier

38
00:01:36,965 --> 00:01:39,020
or hearing or
cognitive impairment.

39
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:42,050
Another tip is to include
alternative text,

40
00:01:42,050 --> 00:01:44,510
also called "alt text," for

41
00:01:44,510 --> 00:01:47,495
any images, drawings,
or diagrams.

42
00:01:47,495 --> 00:01:50,885
Doing so describes the
information relayed in a graphic

43
00:01:50,885 --> 00:01:52,580
to make it accessible to people

44
00:01:52,580 --> 00:01:54,760
who rely on screen readers.

45
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:58,490
To add alt text in Google
Slides or PowerPoint,

46
00:01:58,490 --> 00:02:00,335
simply select the object,

47
00:02:00,335 --> 00:02:03,340
right-click, and then
select "alt text."

48
00:02:03,340 --> 00:02:06,465
The same consideration
applies to charts.

49
00:02:06,465 --> 00:02:08,885
Charts can be
difficult to decipher,

50
00:02:08,885 --> 00:02:10,130
especially if they use

51
00:02:10,130 --> 00:02:12,970
a small font in order
to fit more data.

52
00:02:12,970 --> 00:02:17,085
If your slide includes
data-heavy charts or graphs,

53
00:02:17,085 --> 00:02:19,520
be sure to specify the
takeaway either on

54
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:21,905
the slide itself or
in the speaker notes.

55
00:02:21,905 --> 00:02:25,610
Next, always use text for
critical information.

56
00:02:25,610 --> 00:02:28,175
Never rely only on color or

57
00:02:28,175 --> 00:02:29,780
other visual formatting to convey

58
00:02:29,780 --> 00:02:32,350
critical information
on a chart or slide.

59
00:02:32,350 --> 00:02:35,360
Relying too heavily
on visual formatting

60
00:02:35,360 --> 00:02:37,070
excludes anyone who is

61
00:02:37,070 --> 00:02:39,760
color-blind or unable
to see the screen.

62
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:43,609
For example, to highlight a
new section of a flowchart,

63
00:02:43,609 --> 00:02:46,280
don't simply use a
different color.

64
00:02:46,280 --> 00:02:49,205
Along with the color change,
add a textual cue,

65
00:02:49,205 --> 00:02:50,885
such as the word "new."

66
00:02:50,885 --> 00:02:54,170
If your presentation
relies heavily on images,

67
00:02:54,170 --> 00:02:56,990
consider including a written
summary at the end of

68
00:02:56,990 --> 00:02:58,970
the presentation
so that people can

69
00:02:58,970 --> 00:03:01,885
read your main points
easily in one place.

70
00:03:01,885 --> 00:03:04,550
Another tip is to
include captions for

71
00:03:04,550 --> 00:03:07,025
video content and in real-time.

72
00:03:07,025 --> 00:03:10,730
Provide captions for all
audio or video recordings

73
00:03:10,730 --> 00:03:12,760
shared in your presentation.

74
00:03:12,760 --> 00:03:15,290
If you're using a YouTube video,

75
00:03:15,290 --> 00:03:18,800
check that the YouTube automatic
captioning is accurate.

76
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:20,615
If it's not, request

77
00:03:20,615 --> 00:03:23,285
closed captioning through
a captioning service.

78
00:03:23,285 --> 00:03:25,100
Use real-time captioning for

79
00:03:25,100 --> 00:03:26,990
your presentation, if available.

80
00:03:26,990 --> 00:03:28,730
Along with helping deaf or

81
00:03:28,730 --> 00:03:30,515
hard-of-hearing audience members,

82
00:03:30,515 --> 00:03:32,750
real-time captioning
is useful if there

83
00:03:32,750 --> 00:03:35,165
are diverse accents and
languages in the room,

84
00:03:35,165 --> 00:03:37,805
if the presenter
speaks too quickly,

85
00:03:37,805 --> 00:03:40,610
if there are microphone
issues, or if you

86
00:03:40,610 --> 00:03:41,960
have chatty audience members

87
00:03:41,960 --> 00:03:43,625
who are distracting
their neighbors.

88
00:03:43,625 --> 00:03:45,650
Another thing to remember is for

89
00:03:45,650 --> 00:03:48,785
contrast and text
size, more is better.

90
00:03:48,785 --> 00:03:50,600
The difference between text and

91
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:54,080
its background color is
called contrast ratio.

92
00:03:54,080 --> 00:03:56,780
A high-contrast ratio
makes it easier for

93
00:03:56,780 --> 00:03:59,420
people to read text
or decipher images,

94
00:03:59,420 --> 00:04:01,730
especially if they're
sitting far away

95
00:04:01,730 --> 00:04:04,010
or have low vision
or color blindness.

96
00:04:04,010 --> 00:04:08,215
An ideal contrast ratio is 7:1.

97
00:04:08,215 --> 00:04:10,895
There are contrast checker
tools available online,

98
00:04:10,895 --> 00:04:12,980
so be sure to check those out.

99
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:15,350
Recommendations for text size

100
00:04:15,350 --> 00:04:18,080
vary, but bigger is
usually better.

101
00:04:18,080 --> 00:04:20,090
Before your presentation begins,

102
00:04:20,090 --> 00:04:21,890
go to the back of
the room and make

103
00:04:21,890 --> 00:04:24,055
sure you can read your slides.

104
00:04:24,055 --> 00:04:26,675
Also, the use of
all capital letters

105
00:04:26,675 --> 00:04:29,615
makes reading texts more
difficult for some people,

106
00:04:29,615 --> 00:04:31,820
such as people with dyslexia.

107
00:04:31,820 --> 00:04:34,775
When possible, avoid
using all caps.

108
00:04:34,775 --> 00:04:38,000
This is a simple change
that can go a long way.

109
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:41,620
My final tip is to share
your content in advance.

110
00:04:41,620 --> 00:04:43,940
If possible, send slides to

111
00:04:43,940 --> 00:04:46,940
your audience a few days
before your presentation.

112
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:48,770
This gives the
audience a chance to

113
00:04:48,770 --> 00:04:50,765
review the content, and if needed,

114
00:04:50,765 --> 00:04:52,580
make arrangements to accommodate

115
00:04:52,580 --> 00:04:54,785
their own needs and preferences.

116
00:04:54,785 --> 00:04:56,750
For example, visually-impaired

117
00:04:56,750 --> 00:04:58,760
audience members
might want to follow

118
00:04:58,760 --> 00:05:00,230
along with your slides on

119
00:05:00,230 --> 00:05:03,550
their own device with
screen-reading software.

120
00:05:03,550 --> 00:05:06,770
If you aren't able to share
your slides in advance,

121
00:05:06,770 --> 00:05:08,840
consider sending a document with

122
00:05:08,840 --> 00:05:11,600
a bulleted outline of
your presentation.

123
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:13,580
If you're using acronyms or

124
00:05:13,580 --> 00:05:15,890
technical or obscure terminology,

125
00:05:15,890 --> 00:05:18,485
include a glossary
with definitions.

126
00:05:18,485 --> 00:05:20,860
This information is
especially helpful

127
00:05:20,860 --> 00:05:23,525
for sign language
interpreters and captioners.

128
00:05:23,525 --> 00:05:26,990
All of these best practices
you just learned will help

129
00:05:26,990 --> 00:05:28,670
make your presentations
accessible

130
00:05:28,670 --> 00:05:30,730
to all of your audience members.

131
00:05:30,730 --> 00:05:33,590
Remember, you can't
communicate information

132
00:05:33,590 --> 00:05:37,205
effectively if your audience
can't easily access it.

133
00:05:37,205 --> 00:05:40,190
For these and other best
practices on how to make

134
00:05:40,190 --> 00:05:42,860
your documents and
presentations more accessible,

135
00:05:42,860 --> 00:05:45,130
check out the resources tab.

136
00:05:45,130 --> 00:05:47,300
Coming up, you will learn about

137
00:05:47,300 --> 00:05:49,970
team communication
and best practices.

138
00:05:49,970 --> 00:05:52,070
Good work so far! You're making

139
00:05:52,070 --> 00:05:53,240
real progress, and I'm

140
00:05:53,240 --> 00:05:55,770
excited to be a part
of your journey.
